@Ecore(nsURI="https://cli.models.nasdanika.org", nsPrefix="org.nasdanika.models.cli")
@GenModel(
	modelDirectory="/model/src-gen",
    featureDelegation="Dynamic",
    complianceLevel="25",
    suppressGenModelAnnotations="false",
    copyrightFields="false",
    operationReflection="true",
    importOrganizing="true"
)
/*
 * Nasdanika CLI model - a projection of the picocli (https://picocli.info/) problem
 * domain onto META (https://meta.models.nasdanika.org/), plus the one thing Meta has
 * no reason to have: the resolved command tree.
 *
 * WHY META RATHER THAN A MODEL OF ITS OWN. A picocli command spec is a class with
 * features. `required` and `arity` are lowerBound and upperBound, a multi-valued
 * option is upperBound -1, a Set-typed one is unique, an enum-valued option is a
 * ClassifierReference to a Meta Enum, and an arg group's multiplicity is literally a
 * containment Reference's bounds. Extending Meta is not a stylistic preference: it
 * means picocli-to-JSON-Schema is never written, because picocli-to-Meta is written
 * once here and Meta-to-JSON-Schema is written once there and shared with TypeScript,
 * React and everything else downstream of Meta.
 *
 * THE TYPE / OCCURRENCE SPLIT is the reason this model exists at all. `save` is ONE
 * Command and MANY Registrations - it appears under every html-app producing path in
 * the tree. The generated documentation site cannot state that, because it renders
 * the unfolded tree and duplicates the page at every URL. A Command is a type in the
 * CommandCatalog; a Registration is one place that type occupies in one resolved
 * tree. Everything agent-facing addresses Registrations; everything schema-facing
 * addresses Commands.
 *
 * SNAPSHOTS, NOT DEFINITIONS. The Nasdanika CLI resolves its tree at runtime: the
 * capability framework contributes subcommands and mixins from whatever is on the
 * module layer, so the tree is a property of an INSTALLATION rather than of a
 * release. A Program is therefore a dated snapshot carrying the module list it was
 * resolved from, and comparing two Programs is a supported and useful operation.
 *
 * PROJECTION, NOT MIRROR. Converters, completion candidates, parameter consumers,
 * @Spec injection and heterogeneous factories are not modelled. They land in the
 * inherited Meta `annotations`, on the same bargain Meta strikes with Ecore and XSD:
 * a loader puts what it cannot map there rather than dropping it, so the projection
 * stays honest about being one.
 *
 * EXECUTION IS NOT MODELLED, deliberately and permanently. A command's body is Java
 * reached through a capability requirement. Modelling it rebuilds the MCP model's
 * Binding hierarchy (https://mcp.models.nasdanika.org/), which already exists and is
 * better at it.
 *
 * TOWER POSITION: branches off META for the argument shapes, off the JVM model
 * (https://jvm.models.nasdanika.org/) for provenance, and off the TOOL model
 * (https://tool.models.nasdanika.org/) for the agent surface. The first two are
 * picocli-shaped and the third is not, which is why it is argued separately below
 * under TOOLS. Meta and JVM are
 * parents, neither of which extends the other, and the second one is not decoration:
 * in the Nasdanika CLI a command arrives through a capability factory contribution,
 * so WHICH MODULE PUT IT THERE is the defining fact about it. A command can come
 * from one module, a mixin on that command from a second, and the factory that
 * joined them from a third, and none of those three is derivable from the others.
 * Every JVM reference here is optional and carries a binary name beside it, so a
 * catalog loaded without a JVM model still works and a catalog loaded with one
 * becomes traversable into the module graph.
 *
 * ASSEMBLY. A Program plus the Layer it resolved from is what the packaging story
 * calls an assembly: a chosen set of modules, and therefore a chosen set of
 * commands. This is the model's most useful property and it is worth stating
 * plainly: on this design, WHAT AN AGENT MAY DO IS DECIDED BY WHICH MODULES ARE ON
 * THE PATH. Permission is composition rather than policy, checkable at build time,
 * and enforced by absence rather than by an allowlist something has to evaluate.
 *
 * TOOLS. A command occupying a position in an assembly is a tool by definition, and
 * the tool model already says what a tool is: a description for a model, schemas,
 * examples, and a call with some arguments fixed. So this model sits on Tool rather
 * than restating it. A Command is NOT a tool, it is a type; a CommandTool is the
 * decision to publish one Registration, with a description written for a model and
 * the arguments that must not be model-controlled bound. That keeps being-a-tool a
 * fact about publication rather than about the class, the same split the tool model
 * makes between Operation and OperationCallTool.
 *
 * EXAMPLES come with Tool, from the example model
 * (https://example.models.nasdanika.org/), so this model has no example class of its
 * own. A command's examples are an ExampleSet like anybody else's, which makes them
 * citable, composable with sets outside the CLI, and usable as a conformance suite for
 * the command rather than only as help text.
 *
 * Ownership, lifecycle and governance of a command reach the model the way the
 * TypeScript model gets them, through markers to the elements that actually have
 * owners, not by making every Option Accountable. The one exception argued for
 * below is EffectKind, which is here because a default-deny agent surface needs it
 * before any IAM floor exists.
 *
 * XCORE KEYWORD ESCAPES: `abstract`, `default`, `class` and `interface` are Xcore
 * keywords. Meta's Class and Package are referenced by qualified name for that
 * reason, and because `Class` is ambiguous with java.lang.Class.
 */
package org.nasdanika.models.cli

import org.nasdanika.models.meta.Attribute
import org.nasdanika.models.meta.ModelElement
import org.nasdanika.models.meta.Reference
import org.nasdanika.models.meta.TypeReference

import org.nasdanika.models.jvm.Layer
import org.nasdanika.models.jvm.Type

import org.nasdanika.models.example.ExampleCatalog
import org.nasdanika.models.example.ExampleSet

import org.nasdanika.models.tool.ArgumentBinding
import org.nasdanika.models.tool.BoundTool
import org.nasdanika.models.tool.ToolCatalog

annotation "http://www.eclipse.org/emf/2002/Ecore" as Ecore
annotation "http://www.eclipse.org/emf/2002/GenModel" as GenModel
annotation "urn:org.nasdanika" as Nasdanika

/* ===========================================================================
 * CATALOG - the unit of publication
 * =========================================================================== */

/*
 * A namespace of command types, and the resolved trees built from them.
 *
 * Extends Meta's Package, so `classifiers` holds the Commands, the ArgGroups and the
 * Meta Enums that enum-valued options refer to, and `namespace`, `prefix` and
 * `version` come for free. A consumer that only knows Meta can read the catalog and
 * emit schemas without knowing this model exists, which is the point.
 */
class CommandCatalog extends org.nasdanika.models.meta.Package {
    /*
     * Resolved trees. Normally one, occasionally several: the same catalog compared
     * across installations, or across releases, is how CLI surface drift is found.
     */
    contains Program[] programs
    /*
     * Example catalogs the commands here draw on. Referenced rather than contained,
     * on the same reasoning as ToolCatalog.exampleCatalogs: examples are their own
     * publication unit, and a CLI's examples are as useful to a tool catalog or a
     * conformance run as they are to a help page.
     */
    refers ExampleCatalog[] exampleCatalogs
}

/* ===========================================================================
 * COMMAND TYPES
 * =========================================================================== */

/*
 * A command spec: what picocli builds from a class, independent of where that
 * command sits in any tree.
 *
 * Inherited from Meta Class and used:
 *   name        - the declared name, @Command(name=...)
 *   abstract    - a spec never registered on its own: a mixin, or a base class
 *   superTypes  - Java inheritance between command classes, which picocli honours
 *                 for inherited @Option fields. Genuine inheritance, kept as such.
 *   features    - Options, Positionals and ArgGroupBindings, in declaration order
 *   annotations - everything picocli expresses that this model does not
 * Inherited and deliberately unused: typeParameters, operations. A command's body is
 * not modelled, so there is no signature to carry.
 * Inherited from NxCore Documented: `documentation` is @Command(description=...),
 * and `docRef` is where externally licensed reference text belongs.
 */
class Command extends org.nasdanika.models.meta.Class {
    /* Alternative names the command answers to, @Command(aliases=...). */
    String[] aliases
    /* @Command(header=...): the one line summary, distinct from the description. */
    String header
    /* @Command(customSynopsis=...), when the generated synopsis is overridden. */
    String[] synopsis
    /* Binary name of the Java class the spec was built from. Always set. */
    String sourceTypeName
    /*
     * The same class in a JVM model, when one is loaded beside this catalog. The
     * declaring module is the container chain Type -> Package -> Module, so it is
     * not stored again. Optional: the name above is the authoritative form, the
     * reference is the join.
     */
    refers Type sourceType
    boolean hidden
    /* @Command(helpCommand=true): runs without required arguments being validated. */
    boolean helpCommand
    /*
     * What running this does to the world. UNKNOWN is the default on purpose: an
     * agent-facing surface denies UNKNOWN, so an unclassified command is unreachable
     * rather than silently permitted. Retrofitting this is the expensive order.
     */
    EffectKind effect
    /*
     * Mixin layers, in application order. See MixinBinding: these are layers, not
     * supertypes, and Command.features holds the FLATTENED result.
     */
    contains MixinBinding[] mixins
    /* Subcommands declared statically, @Command(subcommands=...). NOT the resolved
     * tree, which is Registration. A command contributed by a capability provider
     * appears in the tree and never here, and the difference is the whole point. */
    refers Command[] declaredSubcommands
    contains ExitCode[] exitCodes
    /*
     * Worked examples, from the example model. An example's `input` is the command
     * line, program name included, as a user would type it; its `output` is what the
     * command prints, where that is stable enough to state; what it does and why the
     * example exists go in the example's documentation.
     *
     * Examples are the highest value tokens an agent-facing description can spend, and
     * a generated site wants them too. Typed as command lines here and as JSON
     * arguments on a CommandTool, which derives the second from the first: see
     * CommandTool.
     */
    refers ExampleSet[] exampleSets
    /*
     * DECLARATIVE PIPELINES. A command contributes a value its descendants consume
     * (https://medium.com/nasdanika/declarative-command-pipelines-5c70468e7a7e).
     * Stating it as a TypeReference turns "which commands may follow to-model" into
     * a query, and constrains a pipeline an assistant composes by type compatibility
     * rather than by hope. Unset means the command contributes nothing downstream.
     */
    contains TypeReference produces
    /*
     * What an ANCESTOR must produce for this command to be usable. Plural because a
     * command may consume from any ancestor, not only its parent.
     */
    contains TypeReference[] consumes
}

/*
 * A documented exit code, @Command(exitCodeListHeading / exitCodeList).
 *
 * Small, and worth modelling because it is the one part of a CLI contract an
 * automated caller has to interpret and a help page usually buries. Meaning goes in
 * the inherited documentation.
 */
class ExitCode extends ModelElement {
    int code
}

/* ===========================================================================
 * ARGUMENTS
 * =========================================================================== */

/*
 * Common ground between an option and a positional parameter.
 *
 * Extends Meta Attribute rather than Reference because a command line carries values:
 * there is nothing on the other end of an option to point at.
 *
 * Inherited from TypedElement and used:
 *   type        - PrimitiveTypeReference for scalars, ClassifierReference to a Meta
 *                 Enum for enum-valued arguments, OpaqueTypeReference for File, URI,
 *                 and anything with a custom converter
 *   lowerBound  - 0 optional, 1 required. picocli's `required` and the arity lower.
 *   upperBound  - 1 single valued, -1 multi valued. The arity upper.
 *   unique      - the argument collects into a Set
 *   ordered     - collection order is significant
 * Inherited from Feature and used: defaultValue.
 * Inherited and unused: derived, readonly, and Attribute's identifying. A command
 * line has no derived values and nothing identifies an invocation.
 */
abstract class Argument extends Attribute {
    /*
     * picocli's arity string, verbatim: "0..1", "1..*", "2". The bounds above are the
     * parsed form, kept beside it for the same reason the release model parses SemVer
     * into integers: openCypher has no range arithmetic, and the query surface is the
     * reason this model exists.
     */
    String arity
    /* @Option(paramLabel="<file>"), for synopsis rendering. */
    String paramLabel
    boolean hidden
    /* Prompted for rather than read from the command line. Never logged, never in a
     * generated example, and an agent-facing surface must refuse to supply it. */
    boolean interactive
    /*
     * The value must never be persisted: not in telemetry, not in a generated
     * example, not in a saved invocation. `interactive` implies it; this covers the
     * secret passed on the command line or from an environment variable, which
     * picocli has no annotation for.
     *
     * REDACTION IS MODEL DRIVEN because of this flag. A recorder writing a run to
     * disk knows which arguments to drop because the spec says so, rather than by a
     * regular expression over strings that has to guess what a secret looks like.
     * Nothing may be recorded on an agent's branch until this is honoured.
     */
    boolean sensitive
    /* Regular expression splitting one argument into many values. */
    String split
    ArgumentScope scope
    /*
     * Fully qualified ITypeConverter when one is declared. Provenance: the projection
     * emits a string and states the accepted format in documentation, because a JSON
     * schema cannot express a converter.
     */
    String converter
    /*
     * The mixin layer that contributed this argument, unset when it is declared
     * locally. Resolved specs are FLAT - picocli merges mixins at spec build time -
     * so this pointer is what survives the flattening, and it is what makes
     * "every command that gets --verbose, and from which mixin" a one hop query.
     */
    refers MixinBinding contributor
}

/*
 * A named argument. @Option.
 */
class Option extends Argument {
    /* Every form the option answers to: -v, --verbose. The first is canonical. */
    String[] names
    /* @Option(negatable=true): --verbose implies --no-verbose. */
    boolean negatable
    /* Value used when the option is present with no argument of its own. */
    String fallbackValue
}

/*
 * A positional parameter. @Parameters.
 */
class Positional extends Argument {
    /* picocli's index range, verbatim: "0", "1..*". */
    String index
}

/* ===========================================================================
 * GROUPS AND LAYERS
 * =========================================================================== */

/*
 * An argument group. @ArgGroup binds a field whose TYPE holds the arguments, so a
 * group is a class, and extending Meta Class is the literal truth rather than an
 * analogy: `features` holds this group's Options, Positionals and nested
 * ArgGroupBindings.
 *
 * `exclusive` does not become a feature of the projection - it becomes a
 * UnionTypeReference when the group is projected to Meta or to JSON Schema, which is
 * `oneOf`. Keeping it as a boolean here and resolving it in the projection is what
 * keeps the model faithful to picocli while the schema stays faithful to JSON.
 */
class ArgGroup extends org.nasdanika.models.meta.Class {
    /* Members are mutually exclusive. False means all of them apply together. */
    boolean exclusive
    /* @ArgGroup(validate=false): a grouping for help rendering only. */
    boolean validate = "true"
    /* Usage help section heading. */
    String heading
    /* Binary name of the Java class the group was built from. */
    String sourceTypeName
    refers Type sourceType
}

/*
 * A group's appearance on a command or inside another group, and the place where
 * extending Meta pays for itself outright.
 *
 * Extends Meta Reference, so `containment` is true (the group's values are owned by
 * the invocation) and picocli's multiplicity IS lowerBound and upperBound, with no
 * new concept. Lives in Command.features alongside the arguments, in declaration
 * order, so the features list is the whole authoring surface in one place.
 *
 * The inherited `type` is a ClassifierReference to the same ArgGroup that `group`
 * refers to. The duplication is deliberate and follows the release model: a Meta-only
 * consumer reads `type`, a query reads `group` in one hop.
 */
class ArgGroupBinding extends Reference {
    refers ArgGroup group
    /* Verbatim multiplicity, "0..1", "1..*". Bounds are the parsed form. */
    String multiplicity
    int order
}

/*
 * A mixin layer. @Mixin, or a capability contribution.
 *
 * MIXINS ARE LAYERS, NOT SUPERTYPES, and the distinction is the one correction this
 * model makes to the obvious design. Multiple inheritance cannot express three things
 * picocli does: mixins are flattened at spec build time rather than resolved as a
 * diamond, two mixins can collide on an option name, and a mixin is contributed AT
 * RUNTIME by a capability provider, so the set is not a property of the class at all.
 * Layering is: Command.features is the flattened result that a reader consumes, and
 * Argument.contributor points back at the layer that put each one there, which an
 * auditor consumes. The same bargain Docker image layers make, and the same one the
 * object-level inheritance design in the model tower makes.
 *
 * Extends Meta Reference for the same reason ArgGroupBinding does, with the inherited
 * bounds fixed at 1..1: a mixin applies once or not at all.
 */
class MixinBinding extends Reference {
    /* A mixin's spec is itself a CommandSpec, so the target is a Command. */
    refers Command mixin
    /*
     * Capability factory that contributed this layer, as a binary name. Unset for a
     * declared @Mixin.
     *
     * THIS IS THE THREE MODULE CASE, and it is why the JVM dependency is here. The
     * mixin class lives in one module, the command it lands on in another, and this
     * factory in a third. None of the three is derivable from the others, and the
     * question "what on this installation added these options to this command" has no
     * answer anywhere in the source.
     */
    String providerTypeName
    refers Type provider
    /* Application order. Later layers win on a name collision. */
    int order
}

/* ===========================================================================
 * THE RESOLVED TREE
 * =========================================================================== */

/*
 * One CLI, as one installation resolved it, at one moment.
 *
 * `nsd 2026.9.0` with a given set of modules on the layer produces THIS tree; a
 * different set of capability providers produces a different one. That is why a
 * Program is dated and carries its module list rather than being a definition: the
 * snapshot is the unit of comparison, and comparing two of them is how a contributed
 * command that nobody expected gets found.
 *
 * A Program plus its Layer is an ASSEMBLY. Shipping a narrower assembly is how the
 * command set is narrowed, which is why `effects` below is worth computing.
 */
class Program extends ModelElement {
    /* The launcher name, nsd, is the inherited `name`. */
    /* Version reported by the launcher. */
    String version
    /* When the tree was resolved. */
    Date resolved
    /* Names of the modules in the layer the capability loader scanned. Always set. */
    String[] moduleNames
    /*
     * The module layer itself, in a JVM model loaded beside this catalog. The
     * launcher builds its CapabilityLoader over exactly this layer, so the layer is
     * the assembly and the tree below is what the assembly resolved to.
     *
     * Having it as a reference rather than as names is what makes the useful question
     * askable: given these commands, what is the smallest set of modules that still
     * produces them. That is a module graph computation, and the JVM model already
     * carries resolved readability to answer it.
     */
    refers Layer layer
    /*
     * Content hash of the resolved tree.
     *
     * THIS IS WHAT MAKES PER RUN RECORDING TRACTABLE. A thousand runs on one
     * installation resolve the same tree a thousand times, and writing it a thousand
     * times is absurd. Write the Program and its JVM model once per digest, and let
     * every run's telemetry reference the digest. Cardinality drops from per run to
     * per installation, which is how often the fact actually changes.
     *
     * The same value is a change detector, and that is the more interesting use. For
     * a long lived MCP server the tree is resolved per session; a digest that differs
     * between two sessions of one process means the exposed toolset changed while the
     * server was running, which is a security event rather than an optimisation
     * detail. A model that stores the tree per session hides this; one that stores a
     * digest surfaces it for free.
     */
    String digest
    contains Registration root
    /*
     * Every EffectKind occurring anywhere in the tree, computed on load and stored.
     *
     * One attribute read answers "does this assembly touch the network", which is the
     * question an agentic workflow and its reviewer both have to answer, and a
     * traversal is the wrong way to answer something asked on every invocation. An
     * assembly whose effects are READ_ONLY and WRITES_FILES cannot reach past the
     * filesystem, and that is a property of the module path rather than of a policy.
     */
    EffectKind[] effects
}

/*
 * One place a command occupies in one resolved tree.
 *
 * `save` is one Command and many Registrations. Everything agent-facing addresses a
 * Registration, because the question is never "what does save do" but "what does save
 * do HERE, and what has it inherited from the path above it".
 *
 * The inherited `name` is the name at THIS position, which is normally but not
 * necessarily Command.name.
 */
class Registration extends ModelElement {
    /* Alternative names accepted at this position. */
    String[] aliases
    refers Command command
    contains Registration[] subcommands
    /*
     * Space separated path from the program: "nsd drawio to-model html-app save".
     * Computed on load and stored, because it is the identifier every agent-facing
     * surface uses and recomputing it per query is the wrong trade.
     *
     * It is also THE TELEMETRY JOIN KEY. A span named `save` is ambiguous, because
     * `save` occurs at many paths; a span carrying this path is not. The telemetry
     * model resolves semantic convention attributes to model element URIs at load
     * time by design, so `cli.command.path` is all the coupling either model needs,
     * and neither has to depend on the other.
     */
    String path
    /*
     * Binary name of the capability factory that contributed this registration, unset
     * when the subcommand was declared statically. With the JVM reference beside it,
     * "which module contributes this branch of the tree, and what else does that
     * module contribute" is two hops.
     */
    String providerTypeName
    refers Type provider
    boolean hidden
    /*
     * Options declared above this point and visible here: picocli's INHERIT scope.
     * Computed on load and stored, so an assistant authoring at this path is handed
     * one flat option set rather than being asked to walk ancestors correctly.
     */
    refers Option[] inheritedOptions
}

/* ===========================================================================
 * INVOCATION - an authored command line, before it runs
 * =========================================================================== */

/*
 * A command line as a model instance rather than as a string.
 *
 * This is the small class that carries the whole agent argument. A free text command
 * line is the agentic equivalent of concatenated SQL: it can only be checked by
 * running it. An Invocation can be validated against the Registration it targets -
 * required arguments present, types convertible, exclusive groups respected, effect
 * permitted - BEFORE a process starts. That is the same validate-before-run property
 * the Cypher work claims over Groovy evaluators, applied to a command line.
 *
 * Rendering an Invocation to a command line is trivial and one directional. Parsing a
 * string back into one is picocli's job, not this model's.
 */
class Invocation extends ModelElement {
    refers Registration registration
    contains ArgumentValue[] arguments
    /* The next stage of the pipeline: nsd <a> <b> <c> is three nested Invocations. */
    contains Invocation subcommand
}

/*
 * One argument and the values given for it.
 *
 * Values are strings because a command line is strings; conversion is picocli's, and
 * Argument.type is what a validator checks the strings against.
 */
class ArgumentValue extends ModelElement {
    refers Argument argument
    String[] values
}

/* ===========================================================================
 * TOOLS - an assembly's published agent surface
 * =========================================================================== */

/*
 * The tools an assembly publishes.
 *
 * NOT A REPLACEMENT FOR THE THREE FIXED TOOLS. list_commands, describe_command and
 * run_command(path, arguments) remain the general surface, because a tree resolved at
 * runtime cannot be baked into a tool list. This catalog is the curated one: a few
 * registrations, each with a description written for a model and its dangerous
 * arguments bound, validated against a resolved Program rather than baked ahead of
 * one. An assembly may publish either, or both.
 *
 * `tools` is inherited from ToolCatalog and holds CommandTools. Holding anything else
 * is not forbidden, and a catalog mixing command tools with a query tool that
 * ArgumentDiscovery points at is the expected shape.
 */
class CommandToolCatalog extends ToolCatalog {
    /* The assembly snapshot the tools were last validated against. */
    refers Program program
    /*
     * Program.digest at validation time. When the installation's digest differs, the
     * catalog has to be revalidated before it is served: a path that no longer
     * resolves is drift, and a path that resolves to a command with a different spec
     * is worse, because it still looks valid.
     */
    String programDigest
}

/*
 * One registration, published as a tool, with some arguments fixed.
 *
 * Extends BoundTool rather than OperationCallTool: a command has features, not a
 * signature, and binding is what matters about both.
 *
 * ADDRESSED BY PATH, with the reference as the join, on the same bargain as
 * sourceTypeName and sourceType. Programs are snapshots, so an authored tool must
 * outlive the one it was written against; `path` is authoritative and `registration`
 * is resolved into whichever Program is loaded.
 *
 * INPUT SCHEMA, from getInputSchema(), is the Meta projection of the UNBOUND arguments
 * along the whole path: the target Command's features, Registration.inheritedOptions,
 * and the arguments of every ancestor, because running `save` means running
 * `nsd drawio <file> to-model html-app save`. Binding an ancestor's arguments is the
 * main use: fix the input file and the pipeline, and expose only where the output
 * goes. Nothing is hand-written, which is what Meta to JSON Schema is for.
 *
 * VALIDITY, checked on load, because each is cheaper to refuse than to discover:
 *   - every `interactive` argument on the path is bound, since an agent must never be
 *     asked for a prompted value
 *   - every `sensitive` argument on the path is bound, through an evaluator that says
 *     where the value comes from rather than what it is, and never appears in the
 *     schema or in a derived example
 *   - getEffect() is not UNKNOWN, carrying the default-deny rule from Command.effect
 *     into the tool layer
 *
 * EXAMPLES. The inherited `exampleSets` hold examples whose input conforms to the input
 * schema, as the tool model requires. When a tool declares none, it is offered the
 * target command's sets, derived: each command line is parsed against the registration,
 * examples that contradict a binding are dropped, and the unbound values become the
 * JSON arguments. So a command's examples serve its help page and every tool published
 * over it, and a binding that makes an example impossible removes it rather than
 * teaching the model a call it cannot make.
 *
 * INVOCATION. The inherited `invocation` is left unset. How a command runs follows from
 * `path` and the Program, and a second statement of it could only disagree.
 */
class CommandTool extends BoundTool {
    /* Space separated, program name included, as in Registration.path. Always set. */
    String path
    refers Registration registration
    /*
     * The strongest EffectKind anywhere on the path, where UNKNOWN outranks everything.
     * An ancestor that publishes makes the tool one that publishes, however harmless
     * the leaf.
     */
    op EffectKind getEffect()
}

/*
 * A fixed argument somewhere on a command tool's path.
 *
 * A name alone cannot address a command line argument. Options answer to several
 * names, a positional has an index rather than a name, and the same option name can
 * occur on two commands along one path. So the argument is referenced, and its
 * position is a path, with the inherited `parameter` kept as the readable form.
 *
 * The inherited `value` is an Evaluator and the tool model's security note applies:
 * it evaluates at the trust level of the catalog it came from. For a sensitive
 * argument that is the whole point, since the binding says where the secret comes
 * from and the catalog never holds it.
 */
class CommandArgumentBinding extends ArgumentBinding {
    /* Registration path the argument belongs to. Unset means the tool's own path. */
    String path
    refers Registration registration
    refers Argument argument
}

/* ===========================================================================
 * ENUMERATIONS
 * =========================================================================== */

/*
 * What running a command does to the world.
 *
 * UNKNOWN is first so that it is the default literal, and the default matters more
 * than the values: an agent-facing surface denies UNKNOWN, so a command nobody has
 * classified is unreachable rather than quietly permitted. This is a local enum
 * standing in for a proper treatment on the IAM and governance floors, the way the
 * release model's status enum stands in for lifecycle.
 */
enum EffectKind {
    UNKNOWN READ_ONLY WRITES_FILES NETWORK PUBLISHES DESTRUCTIVE
}

/*
 * picocli's ScopeType: whether an argument applies only to its own command or to
 * every command beneath it.
 */
enum ArgumentScope {
    LOCAL INHERIT
}
